WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.052 --> 00:00:01.003
- When I go for something,

00:00:01.003 --> 00:00:03.382
I go for it at the expense
of everything else.

00:00:03.382 --> 00:00:04.784
I always trust my gut, that's all I have.

00:00:04.784 --> 00:00:07.898
Everything is possible
and nothing is guaranteed.

00:00:07.898 --> 00:00:11.606
- Hello, believe nation, it's
Evan, my one word is believe

00:00:11.606 --> 00:00:14.559
and I believe in people more
than they believe in themselves

00:00:14.559 --> 00:00:15.638
and my sincere hope

00:00:15.638 --> 00:00:19.092
is that if you see in
yourself what I see in you,

00:00:19.092 --> 00:00:21.122
you'll be able to change the planet.

00:00:21.122 --> 00:00:22.435
So to help you on your journey,

00:00:22.435 --> 00:00:23.268
today we're going to learn

00:00:23.268 --> 00:00:25.575
from actor and producer Tom Hiddleston

00:00:25.575 --> 00:00:28.743
and my take on his top
10 rules to success.

00:00:28.743 --> 00:00:30.699
Rule number two is my personal favorite

00:00:30.699 --> 00:00:33.115
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:33.115 --> 00:00:34.113
Also as you're watching,

00:00:34.113 --> 00:00:36.121
if you hear something that
really resonates with you

00:00:36.121 --> 00:00:37.898
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:37.898 --> 00:00:40.118
and put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired

00:00:40.118 --> 00:00:41.116
by it as well.

00:00:41.116 --> 00:00:42.921
And if you leave it within
the first couple hours

00:00:42.921 --> 00:00:43.919
of this video going live,

00:00:43.919 --> 00:00:47.035
you have a chance to win
one of two daily prizes.

00:00:47.035 --> 00:00:49.618
(upbeat music)

00:00:59.794 --> 00:01:01.001
- I don't know that I've ever

00:01:01.001 --> 00:01:03.084
been able to do anything.

00:01:05.444 --> 00:01:07.533
I say it as a sort of,

00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:09.314
because I'm aware that
it can be a strength

00:01:09.314 --> 00:01:10.787
but it can also be a weakness

00:01:10.787 --> 00:01:12.721
because the focus is obsessive

00:01:12.721 --> 00:01:15.076
and so when I go for something,

00:01:15.076 --> 00:01:17.162
I go for it at the expense
of everything else.

00:01:17.162 --> 00:01:19.579
And I love what I do, simply,

00:01:24.161 --> 00:01:28.234
and I feel so lucky that
I'm allowed to do it

00:01:28.234 --> 00:01:30.182
and that I get to do it for a living

00:01:30.182 --> 00:01:34.385
and cinema and theater have had
such a profound effect on me

00:01:34.385 --> 00:01:35.700
in my life.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.255
It's just a gut feeling.

00:01:38.255 --> 00:01:40.755
I think I always trust my gut,

00:01:41.922 --> 00:01:45.839
that's all I have, even when I'm working

00:01:45.839 --> 00:01:47.833
and you're thinking about
the shape of a scene

00:01:47.833 --> 00:01:48.836
and how it should be played

00:01:48.836 --> 00:01:51.376
and your head can often get in the way

00:01:51.376 --> 00:01:52.403
and you've got to trust your gut.

00:01:52.403 --> 00:01:54.903
And it's just if I'm inspired,

00:01:58.036 --> 00:02:00.090
if my curiosity is peaked,

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:03.590
and I want to chase this possibility down,

00:02:05.233 --> 00:02:08.768
that I see in front of me on my desk,

00:02:08.768 --> 00:02:10.585
and some of you may have
heard me say this before

00:02:10.585 --> 00:02:12.752
and I'm embarrassed to say

00:02:13.856 --> 00:02:15.188
I always speak in metaphors,

00:02:15.188 --> 00:02:17.887
I don't know how to speak in any other way

00:02:17.887 --> 00:02:22.782
and for me, acting is kind
of like playing the piano

00:02:22.782 --> 00:02:26.142
and if the piano is the human condition

00:02:26.142 --> 00:02:28.892
and we all have every single note

00:02:30.267 --> 00:02:32.767
on that 88 key board within us

00:02:34.083 --> 00:02:38.250
and for me, every role, I want
to be able to play every note

00:02:40.789 --> 00:02:44.039
and sometimes if a character or a story

00:02:45.068 --> 00:02:47.483
seem to stretch me in some way,

00:02:47.483 --> 00:02:51.150
seems to offer up an
experience that is new,

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:54.497
that is a different chord
or a different tune,

00:02:54.497 --> 00:02:58.330
that is real and true
and human and accessible

00:03:00.849 --> 00:03:03.935
and no matter how joyous or loving or warm

00:03:03.935 --> 00:03:05.477
or how dark or painful,

00:03:05.477 --> 00:03:07.915
if it seems like an
interesting shape to inhabit,

00:03:07.915 --> 00:03:11.180
I know if I'm running towards it already.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:12.944
Okay, couple of basic things,

00:03:12.944 --> 00:03:14.512
be on time,

00:03:14.512 --> 00:03:18.641
never underestimate the
importance of punctuality,

00:03:18.641 --> 00:03:20.390
be prepared,

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:21.863
you can never be overprepared

00:03:21.863 --> 00:03:23.780
and be true to yourself

00:03:26.458 --> 00:03:30.484
and if you have something
that you want to say

00:03:30.484 --> 00:03:31.784
that you've been burning to say,

00:03:31.784 --> 00:03:35.966
don't ever let anyone tell
you that you can't say it.

00:03:35.966 --> 00:03:38.565
Everything is possible
and nothing is guaranteed.

00:03:38.565 --> 00:03:41.230
And you start auditioning

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:43.498
and if you're lucky
enough you have an agent

00:03:43.498 --> 00:03:45.587
and you start auditioning
and they send you everything

00:03:45.587 --> 00:03:49.754
from period drama to casualty
to dairy milk commercials,

00:03:52.136 --> 00:03:56.438
yep, I went up for a diary
milk commercial when I was 24

00:03:56.438 --> 00:03:59.021
and you are thrown into the bus

00:04:00.649 --> 00:04:02.251
and thrown into the deep end

00:04:02.251 --> 00:04:06.250
and there's no time for doubt or failure

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:10.224
except routinely that's
what happens to all of us

00:04:10.224 --> 00:04:13.540
and I perhaps was too
careful in auditioning

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:17.290
and I would always get
close to landing roles

00:04:18.544 --> 00:04:19.543
and to booking jobs

00:04:19.543 --> 00:04:22.376
and I would never quite get there.

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:25.562
I mean, this is a universal experience

00:04:25.562 --> 00:04:28.693
for all creative people, certainly actors,

00:04:28.693 --> 00:04:31.033
is the constant rejection at the beginning

00:04:31.033 --> 00:04:33.212
and you have to get used to it

00:04:33.212 --> 00:04:35.280
and you have to get thick skinned about it

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.447
and I got so exhausted by,

00:04:41.074 --> 00:04:42.895
I was trying to sort of

00:04:42.895 --> 00:04:44.621
'cause at first you
take it very personally

00:04:44.621 --> 00:04:48.788
and I thought well, no one's
going to help me get there,

00:04:53.008 --> 00:04:54.741
the only person who can do this is me

00:04:54.741 --> 00:04:58.241
and I started preparing in a different way

00:05:00.946 --> 00:05:05.014
and I sort of stepped up to
the plate a little better,

00:05:05.014 --> 00:05:05.847
I think

00:05:06.722 --> 00:05:08.814
because I could feel time sliding away

00:05:08.814 --> 00:05:11.647
and I wasn't getting on with life,

00:05:13.585 --> 00:05:15.637
I just was sort of stuck in this rut

00:05:15.637 --> 00:05:19.271
of neither acting nor doing anything else.

00:05:19.271 --> 00:05:23.322
And the experience of being
in your 20's is so energetic,

00:05:23.322 --> 00:05:25.342
and I felt so stagnant, actually

00:05:25.342 --> 00:05:29.509
so I just kind of changed
everything up and it worked.

00:05:31.237 --> 00:05:33.755
I think a myth in the business

00:05:33.755 --> 00:05:37.313
is that we all have
everything to choose from.

00:05:37.313 --> 00:05:38.146
We don't.

00:05:39.562 --> 00:05:42.358
I know I'm not the top
of everybody's list.

00:05:42.358 --> 00:05:44.191
No, but that's not it,

00:05:45.112 --> 00:05:46.426
you have to earn that place.

00:05:46.426 --> 00:05:49.415
Matt Damon used to say
every script that he got

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:52.843
he knew it had Brad Pitt's
fingertips all over it

00:05:52.843 --> 00:05:57.010
and mine just happen to be
Michael Fassbenders, that's okay.

00:05:58.729 --> 00:06:01.946
No listen, he's an amazing actor,

00:06:01.946 --> 00:06:04.388
really, truly gifted

00:06:04.388 --> 00:06:06.721
and that was kind of a joke.

00:06:08.579 --> 00:06:11.496
Every actor is completely different

00:06:13.265 --> 00:06:16.160
but you know, sometimes you
might read something and think,

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:18.398
I have read things and
gone, wow this is amazing

00:06:18.398 --> 00:06:21.279
and I've chased it down
and knocked on the door

00:06:21.279 --> 00:06:23.078
and people have said, "It's not you, Tom,"

00:06:23.078 --> 00:06:26.569
or, "We're thinking of someone different."

00:06:26.569 --> 00:06:31.421
And then my job is to keep
knocking on the door and say,

00:06:31.421 --> 00:06:34.004
"Let me see if I can change your mind."

00:06:34.004 --> 00:06:36.108
I think the most enjoyable
thing about acting for me

00:06:36.108 --> 00:06:39.275
is when I sense that in pushing myself

00:06:43.477 --> 00:06:47.477
and challenging myself
to explore new territory,

00:06:50.228 --> 00:06:53.421
when you make a connection with people,

00:06:53.421 --> 00:06:56.807
when people tell you that
they've been moved by something

00:06:56.807 --> 00:06:58.691
or that they related to something,

00:06:58.691 --> 00:07:00.441
it's about connection

00:07:02.797 --> 00:07:04.380
and I find it stems

00:07:07.789 --> 00:07:11.950
from my own passion as an audience member

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:15.033
is I remember my world being changed,

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:17.252
the way I saw the world,

00:07:17.252 --> 00:07:21.428
I remember it being changed
by films and by theater.

00:07:21.428 --> 00:07:25.595
The day that I saw One Flew
Over the Cuckoo's Nest,

00:07:26.794 --> 00:07:28.267
I couldn't stop thinking
about it for weeks

00:07:28.267 --> 00:07:30.767
and I think cinema and theater

00:07:32.544 --> 00:07:33.544
and the same is true,

00:07:33.544 --> 00:07:34.542
I've told this story before

00:07:34.542 --> 00:07:38.956
but I went to see a production
of John Gabriel Borkman

00:07:38.956 --> 00:07:40.769
at the National Theater when I was 14

00:07:40.769 --> 00:07:43.698
and it's the last time that
Paul Scofield was on stage

00:07:43.698 --> 00:07:47.117
and he was with Vanessa
Redgrave and Eileen Atkins

00:07:47.117 --> 00:07:51.284
and I saw, I was very conscious
of an audience of strangers

00:07:52.719 --> 00:07:54.159
arriving at the National Theater

00:07:54.159 --> 00:07:56.207
after a day of doing
whatever they were doing,

00:07:56.207 --> 00:07:57.893
doing their job,

00:07:57.893 --> 00:08:01.623
coming from all quarters
of the city to London,

00:08:01.623 --> 00:08:06.151
to the home of the
theater on the South Bank,

00:08:06.151 --> 00:08:09.858
having a drink, catching up,
rushing in, taking their seats,

00:08:09.858 --> 00:08:10.877
the lights going down

00:08:10.877 --> 00:08:12.276
and three hours later,

00:08:12.276 --> 00:08:14.526
the entire theater sobbing,

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:18.511
wiping away the same tears

00:08:18.511 --> 00:08:20.764
in recognition of the same grief

00:08:20.764 --> 00:08:22.008
which is universal

00:08:22.008 --> 00:08:25.676
and feeling like Paul
Scofield, particularly,

00:08:25.676 --> 00:08:27.564
had spoken to my experience

00:08:27.564 --> 00:08:29.117
and I had never met him

00:08:29.117 --> 00:08:33.133
and I'll always be
grateful to him for that

00:08:33.133 --> 00:08:35.733
and so that's what I love about it really

00:08:35.733 --> 00:08:39.066
is that as I say acting connects people,

00:08:40.529 --> 00:08:42.135
that's what I enjoy.

00:08:42.135 --> 00:08:43.132
And so I always,

00:08:43.132 --> 00:08:47.555
every time a new script
or a new role comes along,

00:08:47.555 --> 00:08:49.605
I'm motivated by the thought

00:08:49.605 --> 00:08:53.772
that I won't forgive myself
if I don't give it everything.

00:08:55.258 --> 00:08:58.329
But I do try to choose
different kinds of things

00:08:58.329 --> 00:09:02.412
because I'm interested in
the diversity of people

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:06.063
and the complexity of people

00:09:07.387 --> 00:09:09.646
and the multiplicity we contain within us.

00:09:09.646 --> 00:09:13.518
I really think the thing that
unites us all in the world

00:09:13.518 --> 00:09:15.819
is that we are constantly inconstant

00:09:15.819 --> 00:09:18.764
and that inconstancy
and that contradiction

00:09:18.764 --> 00:09:20.693
is something that I've
always tried to explore

00:09:20.693 --> 00:09:21.688
with my characters

00:09:21.688 --> 00:09:24.521
because I think we do things that,

00:09:26.195 --> 00:09:28.133
we contradict ourselves all the time.

00:09:28.133 --> 00:09:32.325
There's the Walt Whitman
poem, The Song of Myself,

00:09:32.325 --> 00:09:36.017
"I am large, I contain
multitudes, I contradict myself."

00:09:36.017 --> 00:09:38.166
Learning is the leg work,

00:09:38.166 --> 00:09:42.166
anyone who's been in
anything, a play or a film,

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:47.499
that's our job, we have to
get these words into our head

00:09:47.499 --> 00:09:49.249
and I can't sit down,

00:09:53.343 --> 00:09:55.978
literally I can't sit
down and read something

00:09:55.978 --> 00:09:57.528
and just learn it by rote.

00:09:57.528 --> 00:09:58.888
I have to be on the move.

00:09:58.888 --> 00:10:00.367
So I tend to just,

00:10:00.367 --> 00:10:02.508
I'm the deranged guy in London

00:10:02.508 --> 00:10:05.645
who's walking around
pretending he's on the phone,

00:10:05.645 --> 00:10:09.547
but actually I'm speaking
in iambic pentameter

00:10:09.547 --> 00:10:11.022
'cause I'm learning Shakespeare.

00:10:11.022 --> 00:10:14.878
And it's literally just
repetition, repetition, repetition,

00:10:14.878 --> 00:10:16.113
you kind of drill it into yourself.

00:10:16.113 --> 00:10:17.267
It's like learning,

00:10:17.267 --> 00:10:21.397
I always say that you
have to know the text

00:10:21.397 --> 00:10:24.849
as well as you know Happy Birthday.

00:10:24.849 --> 00:10:25.991
If someone asks you

00:10:25.991 --> 00:10:28.820
to sing a jazzy version of
Happy Birthday you'd know

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:31.211
because you know how Happy Birthday goes

00:10:31.211 --> 00:10:33.427
or if they ask you to
do it like Beethoven,

00:10:33.427 --> 00:10:34.829
you'd be able to do that.

00:10:34.829 --> 00:10:37.255
And it has to be in your body

00:10:37.255 --> 00:10:41.161
with as much kind of confidence as that.

00:10:41.161 --> 00:10:42.961
So it's just repetition really.

00:10:42.961 --> 00:10:45.794
Compassion is about understanding.

00:10:50.921 --> 00:10:53.088
Compassion is without ego,

00:10:55.166 --> 00:10:58.833
if that's possible for
actors, I don't know.

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:03.643
As an actor, to have
compassion as an actor

00:11:05.658 --> 00:11:10.644
is to have compassion for
the characters that I play.

00:11:10.644 --> 00:11:13.609
That's what it means to me.

00:11:13.609 --> 00:11:16.106
And whatever they're going through,

00:11:16.106 --> 00:11:18.439
whatever their predicaments,

00:11:19.448 --> 00:11:21.698
I have to suffer with them.

00:11:22.971 --> 00:11:24.848
I have to understand,

00:11:24.848 --> 00:11:26.765
I have to not judge it.

00:11:28.432 --> 00:11:30.849
I have to be forgiving of it,

00:11:32.085 --> 00:11:36.168
whether it's a circumstance
or their true nature,

00:11:37.489 --> 00:11:40.534
to have compassion for
another human being,

00:11:40.534 --> 00:11:42.873
to have compassion for a character

00:11:42.873 --> 00:11:44.515
is notdifferent from having compassion

00:11:44.515 --> 00:11:45.768
for another human being.

00:11:45.768 --> 00:11:49.935
(upbeat music)
(crowd cheering)

00:11:58.141 --> 00:12:00.759
It was an event in Seoul, in South Korea

00:12:00.759 --> 00:12:02.960
and I was there with the film

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:05.658
and we were, there was a big event

00:12:05.658 --> 00:12:07.155
and there was several
thousand people there

00:12:07.155 --> 00:12:10.624
in a big shopping center
called Times Square

00:12:10.624 --> 00:12:12.766
and I was taking some questions on stage

00:12:12.766 --> 00:12:15.933
and somebody asked in what body part

00:12:15.933 --> 00:12:18.146
are you most confident in?

00:12:18.146 --> 00:12:20.166
Which I thought was a new question,

00:12:20.166 --> 00:12:22.431
one that I hadn't been asked before

00:12:22.431 --> 00:12:25.059
and so I thought the most innocuous answer

00:12:25.059 --> 00:12:26.549
was to say my feet

00:12:26.549 --> 00:12:28.757
because without my feet I couldn't run

00:12:28.757 --> 00:12:29.833
and I love running

00:12:29.833 --> 00:12:33.430
and also I couldn't
dance and I love dancing.

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:35.863
So then they said, "Well, now
we have to see you dance."

00:12:35.863 --> 00:12:38.308
So I thought, oh my God,
I've walked right into this

00:12:38.308 --> 00:12:39.467
but it was fun.

00:12:39.467 --> 00:12:42.050
(upbeat music)

00:12:46.483 --> 00:12:47.484
- Someone told me about it and I was like,

00:12:47.484 --> 00:12:48.549
"Oh no, what's he done?"

00:12:48.549 --> 00:12:50.861
He's a really good dancer, incredible.

00:12:50.861 --> 00:12:51.694
Was that rehearsed?

00:12:51.694 --> 00:12:54.063
That was not an improvised
dance, that one.

00:12:54.063 --> 00:12:55.212
Check it out.

00:12:55.212 --> 00:12:58.306
- I'd like to see that, he's amazing.

00:12:58.306 --> 00:12:59.906
- He's really got it,

00:12:59.906 --> 00:13:01.184
he's really got it.

00:13:01.184 --> 00:13:02.315
He's adorable.

00:13:02.315 --> 00:13:04.898
(upbeat music)

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:13.270
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:14.275
I made this video

00:13:14.275 --> 00:13:17.989
because TellingItLikeItIs
2000 asked me to.

00:13:17.989 --> 00:13:19.189
If there's someone
you'd like me to profile

00:13:19.189 --> 00:13:20.189
in a future top 10,

00:13:20.189 --> 00:13:22.729
please check out the link
down in the description below

00:13:22.729 --> 00:13:25.935
and you can cast your vote
on who we should do next.

00:13:25.935 --> 00:13:27.952
I also want to give a
quick shout out to Tori.

00:13:27.952 --> 00:13:30.456
Tori, thank you so much for
picking up one of my tee shirts,

00:13:30.456 --> 00:13:31.645
the rebel tee shirt,

00:13:31.645 --> 00:13:33.010
it looks great on you

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:34.718
and I hope you're enjoying wearing it out.

00:13:34.718 --> 00:13:36.579
Thank you for sharing the
picture on Instagram for me

00:13:36.579 --> 00:13:38.194
and to your audience as well.

00:13:38.194 --> 00:13:39.457
I really appreciate the support.

00:13:39.457 --> 00:13:40.820
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:42.013
I believe in you.

00:13:42.013 --> 00:13:43.826
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:13:43.826 --> 00:13:45.592
and whatever your one word is.

00:13:45.592 --> 00:13:46.590
Much love.

00:13:46.590 --> 00:13:48.090
I'll see you soon.

00:13:54.231 --> 00:13:57.231
(audience laughter)

00:14:00.879 --> 00:14:02.379
- Don't miss this.

00:14:04.053 --> 00:14:05.711
Does this work out here?

00:14:05.711 --> 00:14:06.544
